resolveLayout (no compaction during interaction, push-down/push-up):

```ts
export function resolveLayout(items: GridItem[], moved: GridItem): GridItem[] {
  let res = items.map(i => i.id === moved.id ? {...moved} : {...i});
  for (let iter = 0; iter < 50; iter++) {
    let any = false;
    const m = res.find(i => i.id === moved.id)!;
    for (const it of res) {
      if (it.id === m.id) continue;
      if (collides(m, it)) {
        const mMid = m.y + m.h / 2, itMid = it.y + it.h / 2;
        if (mMid <= itMid) { it.y = m.y + m.h; }
        else { it.y = Math.max(0, m.y - it.h); }
        any = true;
      }
    }
    if (!any) {
      // also resolve collisions among others (cascade from pushes)
      const sorted = [...res].sort((a,b)=>a.y-b.y||a.x-b.x);
      for (let i=0;i<sorted.length;i++) for (let j=i+1;j<sorted.length;j++) {
        if (collides(sorted[i], sorted[j])) { sorted[j].y = sorted[i].y + sorted[i].h; any = true; }
      }
      res = sorted;
    }
    if (!any) break;
  }
  return res;
}
```

Careful: pushing others up (it.y = m.y - it.h) can cause oscillation with compaction. To keep stable, RGL-style: only push down. But when dragging downward over an item, push-down gives "swap" feel via repeated moves. Actually when you drag down past an item, at some point your item is below theirs, and push-down moves them below you — they jump down a lot. RGL resolves this via collision direction. Push-up when moved is below keeps them above → creates the swap feel. Oscillation risk: A pushed up collides with B above; cascade pushes B down below A... could loop. With the cascade loop sorted by y, pushing colliders down only, plus push-up only for direct collisions with moved item, loops are bounded; iter cap 50 guards. Fine — and final compact() on drop normalizes.

compact:

```ts
export function compact(items: GridItem[]): GridItem[] {
  const sorted = [...items].sort((a,b)=> a.y - b.y || a.x - b.x);
  const out: GridItem[] = [];
  for (const it of sorted) {
    let y = 0;
    // find lowest free position from 0 upward
    const cand = {...it, y: 0};
    while (out.some(p => collides(cand, p))) cand.y++;
    out.push(cand);
  }
  return out;
}
```

This is compact-up-to-fit (each item goes to the highest free slot preserving order). Good.

